    "answer": {
      "short": "The average speed cannot be determined uniquely from the information given.\n\nThe two overlapping interval averages do not determine the displacement change from $t=4$ to $t=6$.",
      "worked": "Let $s(t)$ be the displacement in metres, with $t$ in seconds. The given averages give\n\n$$\\begin{align*}s(4)-s(0)&=8(4)=32\\\\s(6)-s(2)&=10(4)=40\\\\\\text{Average speed on }[4,6]&=\\frac{s(6)-s(4)}{2}\\\\\\text{Average speed on }[4,6]&=\\frac{40+s(2)-s(4)}{2}.\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe change $s(4)-s(2)$ is unknown. Smoothness and increasing displacement do not determine it.\n\nFor example, $s(t)=\\frac{t^2}{2}+6t$ is smooth and increasing on $[0,6]$. It has $s(0)=0$, $s(2)=14$, $s(4)=32$ and $s(6)=54$, so both given averages hold, but the requested average is $\\frac{54-32}{2}=11\\text{ m s}^{-1}$.\n\nAnother smooth, increasing model is $s(t)=\\frac{t^3}{6}-t^2+\\frac{28t}{3}$. It has $s(0)=0$, $s(2)=16$, $s(4)=32$ and $s(6)=56$. Both given averages again hold, but the requested average is $\\frac{56-32}{2}=12\\text{ m s}^{-1}$. Hence there is no unique answer.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
